Running LLMs Locally

When you run an LLM (like LLaMA, Mistral, Phi, or Gemma) on your own machine or server, you’re downloading the model weights and executing inference without needing to call an external API (like OpenAI, Anthropic, or Gemini).

How to run locally:

Install runtimes: Tools like Ollama, LM Studio, or llama.cpp

Hardware: Needs a capable GPU (NVIDIA/AMD) or CPU (Apple M-series also works well).

Process:

Download the model weights (e.g., ollama pull mistral).

Run commands locally (e.g., ollama run mistral "Explain DevOps pipelines").

Integrate into apps via local APIs.

2. Making API Calls to LLMs

Instead of running a model locally, you send a request over the internet to a hosted service (e.g., OpenAI API, Anthropic API, Vertex AI, or AWS Bedrock).

How API calls work:

You write code (Python, Node.js, etc.) that sends prompts to an endpoint.

Example (Python, OpenAI):

from openai import OpenAI
client = OpenAI(api_key="your_api_key")

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="gpt-4o-mini",
    messages=[{"role":"user","content":"Explain Kubernetes in 5 points"}]
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content)

3. Advantages of Each Approach
 Running Locally

Data Privacy – no sensitive info leaves your system.

Cost Control – free after setup (no API usage charges).

Customization – fine-tune models, control inference parameters deeply.

Offline Use – works without internet.

 Using APIs

Ease of Use – no need for powerful GPUs or setup.

Access to SOTA Models – APIs usually provide the latest & largest models (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini).

Scalability – cloud handles heavy compute and scaling.

Maintenance-Free – no need to update weights or optimize performance.

:: Rule of thumb:

If you’re experimenting, handling sensitive data, or want low cost → run locally.

If you need the latest, largest, most accurate models with minimal setup → use APIs.

Many teams combine both: local models for internal debugging, APIs for production-grade tasks.
